{"exhaustive":{"nbHits":false,"typo":false},"exhaustiveNbHits":false,"exhaustiveTypo":false,"hits":[{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"soasme"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["federal","reserve"],"value":"Small Python (spy) is a small, embeddable Python engine written in C11. Pretty much like quickjs for javascript, it's for Python.<p>The entire Python core language features is full implemented with almost all builtins. No Lib/ is included except sys/gc that changes the interpreter behaviors.<p>Is missing Python stdlib a pain? Probably no. Host injected modules allows any language's ecosystem to be used in Python syntax. Check examples, you can build game by using Raylib, or build web <em>serve</em>r using Axum/Serde_python (using Rust crates).<p>Just copy `spy.h`, `spyc-*.c` into your project and use it as a C library. Or <em>several</em> bindings are provided for languages like Js, Python, Rust, Go. You can install via pip, npm, crate, go. Worker mode is recommended so that any issue would be contained in a subprocess.<p>It's fast. Well, not at the speed of Pypy because it's not JIT. But it's slightly faster than C Python in most benched cases.<p>Another good feature is compiling Python into a binary. You could use PyInstaller, but it's gonna be so large. Small Python's binary is small. A raylib + spy based PONG game is built at around 2MB.<p>It's using a full suite of tests: unittest, fuzz, sanitize, bench, TLA+ specs. Despite using C, agents are unable to spot a single segfault as of now. So that's pretty cool. :)<p>The roadmap aims to build it for agents: snapshot/resume between durable sessions, policy declaration so agents never shoot on your feet, etc.<p>It has been built for one week. I have a long Claude Code session running over <em>several</em> days with /remote-control using Opus 5.5.<p>Repo url: <a href=\"https://github.com/soasme/small-python\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://github.com/soasme/small-python</a>\nPrevious HN post: <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50281226\">https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50281226</a>"},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Show HN: A faster Python 3 interpreter implemented in C11, one week"}},"_tags":["story","author_soasme","story_50330796","show_hn"],"author":"soasme","children":[50331238],"created_at":"2026-11-01T23:00:12Z","created_at_i":1793574012,"num_comments":1,"objectID":"50330796","points":1,"story_id":50330796,"story_text":"Small Python (spy) is a small, embeddable Python engine written in C11. Pretty much like quickjs for javascript, it&#x27;s for Python.<p>The entire Python core language features is full implemented with almost all builtins. No Lib&#x2F; is included except sys&#x2F;gc that changes the interpreter behaviors.<p>Is missing Python stdlib a pain? Probably no. Host injected modules allows any language&#x27;s ecosystem to be used in Python syntax. Check examples, you can build game by using Raylib, or build web server using Axum&#x2F;Serde_python (using Rust crates).<p>Just copy `spy.h`, `spyc-*.c` into your project and use it as a C library. Or several bindings are provided for languages like Js, Python, Rust, Go. You can install via pip, npm, crate, go. Worker mode is recommended so that any issue would be contained in a subprocess.<p>It&#x27;s fast. Well, not at the speed of Pypy because it&#x27;s not JIT. But it&#x27;s slightly faster than C Python in most benched cases.<p>Another good feature is compiling Python into a binary. You could use PyInstaller, but it&#x27;s gonna be so large. Small Python&#x27;s binary is small. A raylib + spy based PONG game is built at around 2MB.<p>It&#x27;s using a full suite of tests: unittest, fuzz, sanitize, bench, TLA+ specs. Despite using C, agents are unable to spot a single segfault as of now. So that&#x27;s pretty cool. :)<p>The roadmap aims to build it for agents: snapshot&#x2F;resume between durable sessions, policy declaration so agents never shoot on your feet, etc.<p>It has been built for one week. I have a long Claude Code session running over several days with &#x2F;remote-control using Opus 5.5.<p>Repo url: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;soasme&#x2F;small-python\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;soasme&#x2F;small-python</a>\nPrevious HN post: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&#x2F;item?id=50281226\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&#x2F;item?id=50281226</a>","title":"Show HN: A faster Python 3 interpreter implemented in C11, one week","updated_at":"2026-11-01T23:56:41Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"lastmjs"},"story_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["federal","reserve"],"value":"Lasm has been my dream for a good part of 2026.\nI firmly believe that formal verification is the next evolution of software engineering and programming languages. This next wave will largely be enabled by the incredibly intelligent LLMs that understand formal verification and its programming languages.<p>Lean is one of the most popular languages that allow for formal verification, and to me is incredibly powerful because it is a <em>general</em> purpose programming language with formal verification built directly into it.<p>You can build <em>general</em> purpose software in Lean, and formally verify it in Lean. Amazing.<p>Lasm brings Lean to the browser, Node, <em>serve</em>rless, and edge environments (and more). The idea is that you can now write web apps, frontend to backend, in Lean and formally verify these apps.<p>Imagine an Express <em>serve</em>r or Hono <em>serve</em>r, running on Cloudflare Workers, with formal verification of its specification.<p>I'm an agentic engineer with an emphasis in software engineering, and Lasm was built mostly by Claude Opus 5.5 with my high-level direction."},"title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"partial","matchedWords":["reserve"],"value":"Show HN: Lasm \u2013 Write formally verified webapps in Lean for Node/<em>serve</em>rless/edge"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://github.com/Millillion/lasm"}},"_tags":["story","author_lastmjs","story_50299004","show_hn"],"author":"lastmjs","children":[50300120,50300430,50300076,50301194,50299954,50301450,50300742,50303944],"created_at":"2026-10-30T02:05:41Z","created_at_i":1793325941,"num_comments":22,"objectID":"50299004","points":38,"story_id":50299004,"story_text":"Lasm has been my dream for a good part of 2026.\nI firmly believe that formal verification is the next evolution of software engineering and programming languages. This next wave will largely be enabled by the incredibly intelligent LLMs that understand formal verification and its programming languages.<p>Lean is one of the most popular languages that allow for formal verification, and to me is incredibly powerful because it is a general purpose programming language with formal verification built directly into it.<p>You can build general purpose software in Lean, and formally verify it in Lean. Amazing.<p>Lasm brings Lean to the browser, Node, serverless, and edge environments (and more). The idea is that you can now write web apps, frontend to backend, in Lean and formally verify these apps.<p>Imagine an Express server or Hono server, running on Cloudflare Workers, with formal verification of its specification.<p>I&#x27;m an agentic engineer with an emphasis in software engineering, and Lasm was built mostly by Claude Opus 5.5 with my high-level direction.","title":"Show HN: Lasm \u2013 Write formally verified webapps in Lean for Node/serverless/edge","updated_at":"2026-11-01T22:44:55Z","url":"https://github.com/Millillion/lasm"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"toomuchtodo"},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"The Fed Faces Its Most Confusing Economy Yet"},"url":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["federal","reserve"],"value":"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-10-23/<em>federal</em>-<em>reserve</em>-interest-rate-decision-faces-challenges-from-ai-inflation-war"}},"_tags":["story","author_toomuchtodo","story_50224565"],"author":"toomuchtodo","children":[50224766,50224566],"created_at":"2026-10-24T19:59:35Z","created_at_i":1792871975,"num_comments":2,"objectID":"50224565","points":3,"story_id":50224565,"title":"The Fed Faces Its Most Confusing Economy Yet","updated_at":"2026-10-28T11:31:41Z","url":"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-10-23/federal-reserve-interest-rate-decision-faces-challenges-from-ai-inflation-war"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"toomuchtodo"},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"How the Fed Is Quietly Inflating the AI Bubble While Hiking Your Electric Bill"},"url":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["federal","reserve"],"value":"https://www.thebignewsletter.com/p/how-the-<em>federal</em>-<em>reserve</em>-secretly"}},"_tags":["story","author_toomuchtodo","story_50142772"],"author":"toomuchtodo","children":[50144093,50144513,50144267,50145168,50148785,50241202,50144136],"created_at":"2026-10-18T16:48:25Z","created_at_i":1792342105,"num_comments":14,"objectID":"50142772","points":30,"story_id":50142772,"title":"How the Fed Is Quietly Inflating the AI Bubble While Hiking Your Electric Bill","updated_at":"2026-10-29T15:50:46Z","url":"https://www.thebignewsletter.com/p/how-the-federal-reserve-secretly"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"7777777phil"},"title":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["federal","reserve"],"value":"The Most Dangerous Idea in <em>Federal</em> <em>Reserve</em> History"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://www.aeaweb.org/conference/2013/retrieve.php?pdfid=466"}},"_tags":["story","author_7777777phil","story_50109410"],"author":"7777777phil","children":[50109918],"created_at":"2026-10-15T19:58:42Z","created_at_i":1792094322,"num_comments":1,"objectID":"50109410","points":1,"story_id":50109410,"title":"The Most Dangerous Idea in Federal Reserve History","updated_at":"2026-10-15T20:43:03Z","url":"https://www.aeaweb.org/conference/2013/retrieve.php?pdfid=466"}],"hitsPerPage":5,"nbHits":8,"nbPages":2,"page":0,"params":"query=Federal+Reserve&tags=story&numericFilters=created_at_i%3E1790994428%2Ccreated_at_i%3C1793586428&hitsPerPage=5&advancedSyntax=true&analyticsTags=backend","processingTimeMS":12,"processingTimingsMS":{"_request":{"queue":1,"roundTrip":21},"fetch":{"query":10,"total":11},"total":12},"query":"Federal Reserve","serverTimeMS":14}
